#ifndef RBTREE_H
#define RBTREE_H
#include <stddef.h>
typedef struct rbtree rbtree_t;
typedef void (*rb_value_free_fn)(void *value);
/* Returns NULL on allocation failure.
* value_free may be NULL (values not owned). */
rbtree_t *rb_create(rb_value_free_fn value_free);
/* Copies key (tree owns the copy).
* Takes ownership of value ONLY on success.
* Overwriting an existing key frees the old value via value_free.
* Returns 0 on success, -1 on allocation failure (tree unchanged, value NOT
* consumed; caller still owns it). */
int rb_insert(rbtree_t *t, const char *key, void *value);
/* Returns the value, or NULL if absent. Tree retains ownership. */
void *rb_find(const rbtree_t *t, const char *key);
/* Removes key; frees the key copy and the value.
* Returns 0, or -1 if absent. */
int rb_delete(rbtree_t *t, const char *key);
size_t rb_size(const rbtree_t *t);
/* In-order traversal. */
void rb_foreach(const rbtree_t *t,
void (*fn)(const char *key, void *value, void *ctx),
void *ctx);
/* Returns 0 iff all red-black invariants hold
* (see the baseline section). */
int rb_validate(const rbtree_t *t);
/* Frees all nodes, key copies, and (if owned) values. NULL-safe. */
void rb_destroy(rbtree_t *t);
#endif

input.prompt:
We are planning a from-scratch C23 implementation of a red-black tree for a course lab (rbtree-lab). All source/test files are currently EMPTY skeletons — this is a green-field design, not a modification of existing code. Produce a detailed implementation plan (not code) covering data structures, algorithms, and test strategy.

## The graded contract (include/rbtree.h — MUST NOT be modified, this is fixed)

```c
#ifndef RBTREE_H
#define RBTREE_H
#include <stddef.h>
typedef struct rbtree rbtree_t;
typedef void (*rb_value_free_fn)(void *value);
/* Returns NULL on allocation failure.
 * value_free may be NULL (values not owned). */
rbtree_t *rb_create(rb_value_free_fn value_free);
/* Copies key (tree owns the copy).
 * Takes ownership of value ONLY on success.
 * Overwriting an existing key frees the old value via value_free.
 * Returns 0 on success, -1 on allocation failure (tree unchanged, value NOT
 * consumed; caller still owns it). */
int rb_insert(rbtree_t *t, const char *key, void *value);
/* Returns the value, or NULL if absent. Tree retains ownership. */
void *rb_find(const rbtree_t *t, const char *key);
/* Removes key; frees the key copy and the value.
 * Returns 0, or -1 if absent. */
int rb_delete(rbtree_t *t, const char *key);
size_t rb_size(const rbtree_t *t);
/* In-order traversal. */
void rb_foreach(const rbtree_t *t,
    void (*fn)(const char *key, void *value, void *ctx),
    void *ctx);
/* Returns 0 iff all red-black invariants hold
 * (see the baseline section). */
int rb_validate(const rbtree_t *t);
/* Frees all nodes, key copies, and (if owned) values. NULL-safe. */
void rb_destroy(rbtree_t *t);
#endif
```

## Project rules (from CLAUDE.md, already established, do not re-derive — just design within them)
- Build: `make test` runs unit tests (build/test_rbtree) AND a fuzz binary (build/fuzz 100000). `make asan` = same test target rebuilt with -fsanitize=address,undefined. `make memcheck` runs valgrind --leak-check=full --show-leak-kinds=all --error-exitcode=1 on both the test binary and `./build/fuzz 20000`.
- A change is done only when unit tests + asan + memcheck all pass.
- NEVER modify include/rbtree.h.
- Every allocation must be checked; a NULL malloc/strdup-equivalent must leave the tree completely unchanged and return the documented error code (this is the hard part: rb_insert must be exception-safe / transactional on allocation failure, including on the "overwrite existing key" path where the OLD value must not be freed and the tree must not be mutated until we know the new value's storage succeeded... actually re-read the contract: overwriting an existing key DOES free the old value via value_free — but only on success, since on allocation failure the tree must be unchanged. Nail down exactly when allocation failures can occur (key copy for a new node, node struct alloc) and ensure the value's ownership transfer and old-value freeing only happen after all allocations that could fail have already succeeded).
- Never weaken/skip/delete tests to make suite pass.
- C23, -Wall -Wextra -Werror clean, no VLAs.
- goto-cleanup pattern for multi-allocation functions.
- Smallest diff that passes; no unrelated refactors.
- Every non-obvious loop needs a one-line invariant comment.
- Files to produce: src/rbtree.c (implementation), tests/test_rbtree.c (unit tests using assert-style checks, must exercise insert/find/delete/overwrite/size/foreach/validate/destroy including allocation-failure simulation if feasible, e.g. via a malloc wrapper/interposition or a testable hook), tests/fuzz.c (a fuzz driver taking an iteration count argv[1], doing randomized insert/delete/find against both the rbtree and a reference model (e.g. a simple linked list or qsort'd array) and calling rb_validate() after every mutation, then rb_destroy() at the end).

## What the plan must cover
1. Internal node/tree struct design (colors, parent pointers or not, sentinel nil node vs NULL leaves — pick one and justify), and how key storage (owned copy) and value storage (conditionally owned) are represented.
2. Full algorithm plan for: create, insert (BST insert + insert-fixup with the 3 standard cases, referencing CLRS-style case numbering), find, delete (BST delete with successor splicing + delete-fixup with the 4 standard cases including the sibling color/children-color case analysis), size (O(1) counter maintained incrementally, not O(n) count), foreach (recursive or explicit-stack in-order traversal), validate (check BST ordering, check no red node has a red child, check every root-to-leaf black-height is equal, check root is black), destroy (post-order free, NULL-safe entry).
3. Precise allocation-failure semantics for insert: exactly what gets allocated (node struct, key copy via a malloc'd duplicate since we can't assume POSIX strdup is available/portable under -std=c23 strictly — decide whether to hand-roll a copy), in what order, and how a mid-way failure unwinds via goto-cleanup so the tree is provably unchanged and the caller's value is provably not consumed. Cover both the "new key" path and the "overwrite existing key" path distinctly.
4. How rb_foreach's fn signature (const char *key, void *value, void *ctx) maps to in-order traversal without exposing internal node pointers.
5. Test strategy for tests/test_rbtree.c: enumerate concrete test cases (empty tree ops, single insert/find/delete, overwrite semantics, delete-absent-key returns -1, size tracking across many inserts/deletes/duplicate overwrites, foreach visits in sorted order, validate on trees built by many random insert/delete sequences, NULL value_free vs non-NULL value_free ownership behavior, rb_destroy on NULL, rb_destroy freeing values via value_free). Also design an allocation-failure test path: propose a concrete mechanism (e.g. a test-only function pointer for malloc that test_rbtree.c can override before including rbtree.c directly, OR a compile-time injected failure counter) — CLAUDE.md requires NULL-return allocation checks to be actually verified by a test, not just eyeballed, so the plan must include a concrete way to trigger and assert on that path without modifying rbtree.h.
6. Fuzz driver design (tests/fuzz.c): random op mix, reference model choice, how it detects divergence, how rb_validate is woven in, exit codes.
7. Sequencing: what order to write/build/test the pieces in so `make test` stays buildable throughout (e.g., build create/destroy/insert/find first with a trivial always-red-root, no fixup, get it compiling and passing basic tests, THEN add insert-fixup, THEN delete+delete-fixup, THEN validate, THEN wire up the fuzz harness) — but note the final diff should still be reviewed as a whole per "smallest diff that passes" (this refers to avoiding unrelated scope creep, not literally one commit — clarify commit granularity against CLAUDE.md's "M<n>: <what>" commit message convention, suggesting logical milestlike commit boundaries).

Do not write any code. Output a structured implementation plan with concrete file-by-file responsibilities, key function signatures (internal, static — since rbtree.h is fixed), and the test list. Flag any open design decisions that need a human/user call (e.g., sentinel-nil vs NULL-leaf representation, how to simulate malloc failure in tests) as explicit questions rather than silently picking one.

